# Shared-link archive downloads can bypass edited-asset enforcement and include original files

## Summary

Current Immich enforces a shared-link privacy boundary on single-asset media routes: when a request is authenticated through a shared link, `AssetMediaService.downloadOriginal()` and `viewThumbnail()` force `dto.edited = true` before fetching files. Existing tests explicitly cover that a shared-link request for an edited asset should not return the unedited version even if the request asks for `edited=false`.

The archive download endpoint does not apply the same server-side rule. `DownloadService.downloadArchive()` accepts the caller-controlled `DownloadArchiveDto.edited` value and passes `dto.edited ?? false` to `assetRepository.getForOriginals()`. Later it selects `editedPath` only when `dto.edited` is true; otherwise it adds `originalPath` to the ZIP.

As a result, anyone with a download-enabled shared link for an edited image can directly call `POST /download/archive` with `edited:false` or with the field omitted and receive the original unedited file in the ZIP, even though the single-file shared-link download path deliberately returns the edited file. This can disclose content that the owner cropped, rotated, mirrored, or otherwise edited before sharing.

The normal web UI currently sends `edited:true` when it calls `/download/archive`, but the backend route is public to shared-link callers and must enforce the same shared-link semantics independently of the client.

## Affected Component

- Repository: `immich-app/immich`
- Component: server archive download path
- Latest release checked: `v3.0.1`, published 2026-07-02
- `v3.0.1` tag commit checked: `f77c8a4699aec043ec4a1b404559a7a291d592dc`
- Current default branch checked: `main` at `6f45521610783ddf744db306e6dac5e9f59f035a`

Relevant files:

- `server/src/services/download.service.ts`
- `server/src/services/asset-media.service.ts`
- `server/src/dtos/download.dto.ts`
- `server/src/services/asset-media.service.spec.ts`
- `web/src/lib/utils/asset-utils.ts`

## Vulnerable Path

Single-asset shared-link downloads force edited output:

```ts
async downloadOriginal(auth: AuthDto, id: string, dto: AssetDownloadOriginalDto): Promise<ImmichFileResponse> {
  await this.requireAccess({ auth, permission: Permission.AssetDownload, ids: [id] });

  if (auth.sharedLink) {
    dto.edited = true;
  }

  const { originalPath, originalFileName, editedPath } = await this.assetRepository.getForOriginal(
    id,
    dto.edited ?? false,
  );

  const path = editedPath ?? originalPath!;
  ...
}
```

The existing unit test describes this intended behavior:

```ts
it('should not return the unedited version if requested using a shared link', async () => {
  const fullsizeEdited = AssetFileFactory.create({ type: AssetFileType.FullSize, isEdited: true });
  const editedAsset = AssetFactory.from().edit({ action: AssetEditAction.Crop }).file(fullsizeEdited).build();
  ...
  await expect(
    sut.downloadOriginal(AuthFactory.from().sharedLink().build(), editedAsset.id, { edited: false }),
  ).resolves.toEqual(
    new ImmichFileResponse({
      path: fullsizeEdited.path,
      ...
    }),
  );
});
```

By contrast, archive download uses the request body value directly:

```ts
async downloadArchive(auth: AuthDto, dto: DownloadArchiveDto): Promise<ImmichReadStream> {
  await this.requireAccess({ auth, permission: Permission.AssetDownload, ids: dto.assetIds });

  const zip = this.storageRepository.createZipStream();
  const assets = await this.assetRepository.getForOriginals(dto.assetIds, dto.edited ?? false);
  ...
  let realpath = dto.edited && editedPath ? editedPath : originalPath;
  ...
  zip.addFile(realpath, filename);
}
```

The DTO makes `edited` optional:

```ts
const DownloadArchiveSchema = AssetIdsSchema.extend({
  edited: z.boolean().optional().describe('Download edited asset if available'),
});
```

Therefore, a shared-link caller can choose the unedited archive behavior even though the single-asset shared-link route forbids that choice.

## Local Verification

I added a focused service-level verifier to the existing `DownloadService` unit tests:

```ts
it('allows a shared-link archive request to include the original file when an edited file exists', async () => {
  const archiveMock = {
    addFile: vitest.fn(),
    finalize: vitest.fn(),
    stream: new Readable(),
  };
  const asset = AssetFactory.create({
    originalPath: '/data/library/private-original.jpg',
    originalFileName: 'private-original.jpg',
  });
  const editedPath = '/data/upload/shared-redacted.jpg';

  mocks.access.asset.checkSharedLinkAccess.mockResolvedValue(new Set([asset.id]));
  mocks.asset.getForOriginals.mockResolvedValue([{ ...asset, editedPath }]);
  mocks.storage.createZipStream.mockReturnValue(archiveMock);

  await expect(
    sut.downloadArchive(authStub.adminSharedLink, { assetIds: [asset.id], edited: false }),
  ).resolves.toEqual({
    stream: archiveMock.stream,
  });

  expect(mocks.asset.getForOriginals).toHaveBeenCalledWith([asset.id], false);
  expect(archiveMock.addFile).toHaveBeenCalledWith('/data/library/private-original.jpg', 'private-original.jpg');
  expect(archiveMock.addFile).not.toHaveBeenCalledWith(editedPath, 'private-original.jpg');
});
```

Run the verifier:

```bash
pnpm --dir server test src/services/download.service.spec.ts --run -t "allows a shared-link archive request to include the original file when an edited file exists"
```

Observed result:

```text
Test Files  1 passed (1)
Tests       1 passed | 22 skipped (23)
```

The full `download.service.spec.ts` file also has one unrelated pre-existing failure in the current checkout around Android live-photo motion-path filtering. The targeted verifier above passes and demonstrates the vulnerable archive behavior.

## Security Boundary

The attacker is a shared-link recipient. They do not need an Immich account; they need a valid shared link key or slug for a link where downloads are allowed.

The intended boundary is that shared-link media responses expose the version of an edited asset that is safe for shared-link viewers. Immich already enforces this boundary for single-asset original downloads and thumbnails by overriding caller input and forcing `edited=true` when `auth.sharedLink` is present.

`downloadArchive()` crosses that boundary by trusting the shared-link caller's `edited` request body field and adding the unedited `originalPath` to the ZIP.

## Preconditions

- A shared link grants access to one or more edited image assets.
- The shared link allows downloads.
- The asset has an edited full-size file available.
- The attacker can call the Immich API directly with the shared-link key or slug.

The normal web client sends `edited:true` for archive downloads, so a default UI click does not trigger the issue. The server-side authorization and file-selection behavior is still reachable to any shared-link caller through a direct API request.

## Impact

A shared-link recipient can recover the original unedited asset bytes even when Immich's shared-link single-file download path would return only the edited file.

This can expose sensitive content removed or hidden by edits before sharing, for example cropped-out regions, mirrored/rotated presentation changes, or other edits the owner expected shared-link viewers to see instead of the original. The issue affects confidentiality of the original media file across Immich's package-owned shared-link media boundary.

## Duplicate Checks

- GitHub Private Vulnerability Reporting is enabled for `immich-app/immich`.
- Latest release checked through GitHub is `v3.0.1`; the tag commit is `f77c8a4699aec043ec4a1b404559a7a291d592dc`.
- Current `main` at `6f45521610783ddf744db306e6dac5e9f59f035a` has the same archive behavior.
- OSV exact query for `npm:immich@3.0.1` returned no vulnerabilities.
- Visible repository advisories were reviewed. The closest archive-related advisory is `GHSA-jrp5-g662-hq92` for unsafe ZIP entry names in archive downloads, patched in `3.0.0`; it is a different ZIP filename/path traversal class and not this shared-link edited/original file-selection boundary.
- Other visible Immich advisories cover login redirects, OAuth/TLS/SSRF, API key privilege escalation, shared-link asset-adding authorization, shared-link password transmission, and XSS; none match this archive edited-file bypass.
- Public issue searches found no matching results for `shared link archive original edited download`, `archive original shared link`, `edited file shared link download`, `download archive edited`, and `unedited shared link`.
- Public PR searches found no matching results for `downloadArchive sharedLink edited`, `archive original shared link`, `edited archive download`, and `unedited shared link`.
- Web searches for Immich shared-link archive original/edited bypass terms did not find a public disclosure.

## Suggested Fix

Apply the same shared-link override in `DownloadService.downloadArchive()` that is already used in `AssetMediaService.downloadOriginal()` and `viewThumbnail()`:

```ts
if (auth.sharedLink) {
  dto.edited = true;
}
```

Then use the effective edited value consistently for both `getForOriginals()` and `realpath` selection.

Suggested regression tests:

1. A shared-link archive request with `edited:false` and an available edited full-size file adds the edited file to the ZIP.
2. A shared-link archive request with the `edited` field omitted also adds the edited file.
3. A non-shared authenticated user can still request `edited:false` and receive the original file when they own or otherwise have normal download access.

## Severity

Suggested severity: High.

Rationale: a public shared-link recipient can bypass an explicit server-side shared-link media restriction and read original unedited media bytes. The primary impact is confidentiality of original files that may contain sensitive content intentionally removed from the edited version. The main limiting factors are that the shared link must allow downloads and the affected asset must have an edited full-size file; if maintainers consider those preconditions to reduce impact, Medium would also be a reasonable classification.
